Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A friendship between two people who are of equal status ought to be based on equality. This means that each persons renders the same amount of love, respect, and service to the other.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle distinguishes between friendships between equals and unequals and this addresses the former case.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality\">A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality</a><ul><li>The content of this note is contrasted with this other note.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI represents a new epistemological category":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>AI represents something that is entirely new compared to past innovations (in terms of epistemology) of humans. This manifests both in its potential upside and downside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents AI as something that is entirely new in terms of the epistemology that it represents.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The \"magics\" referenced here are to broad, informational improvements that humanity has experienced, not specific technological advancements. Humanity's first \"magic\" is history, a means of storing information which meant that information could be passed down to future generations and thus preserved with detail. Humanity's second \"magic\" is science, a means of making observations of nature rigorous and testable, creating generalizable laws of nature in the process. Humanity's third \"magic\" is AI, which is able to take in massive amounts of data and come to some sort of conclusion, despite there being no discernable generalizable laws that are formulated in the process.</p><ul><li>While this gives us a huge amount of power in that it allows us to find regularities in complex systems that we would not otherwise be able to find, it also means that internal mechanisms of AI are not able to be comprehended by humans.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>Perhaps another examples/case study of humans engagement with AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI will reduce wage inequality through 'simplification'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While many people have talked about how AI could potentially increase inequality, evidence suggests that AI could likewise provide something to the opposite effect.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disputes the narrative that AI can only increase inequality and argues for why it will benefit the average worker.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>21% average wage growth, with gains concentrated at the bottom of the income distribution.</li><li>26-34% welfare impact (equivalent variation, or permanent proportional wage increase) across all occupations and skill levels.<ul><li>The average is estimated at 21%, and this is lower because AI disproportionately affects lower-skill workers.</li></ul></li><li>AI helps through:<ul><li>Augmentation: it saves 20.2% worker time on average.</li><li>Automation: 22% of tasks can be fully automated.</li><li>Simplification (most important): it lowers the required skill level by 18.3%.<ul><li>It lowers the barrier to entry for complex tasks, leading to wage compression and thus the scarcity premium for experts decreases.</li><li>This is also the key aspect that causes inequality to decrease in the presence of AI. While augmentation drives the average wage boost, it is simplification that drives the distribution.</li><li>Simplification means that the value shifts from “knowing how to do something” to “knowing what to do.”</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>It especially helps bridge the gap between experts and novices in verbal-heavy domains. This means that math and social skills become the new “defensible moats.”</li><li>Generative AI reduces inequality due to simplification, through generative AI + physical AI means that inequality would increase, since that would lead to mass automation.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that although this is the case, it could potentially make labor markets more competitive, which would generally just lower wages. Like if everybody becomes AI enhanced, even if the service they provide has a “human premium” (e.g., a therapist), there remain ways to augment the service that the human provides such that it is easier for others to enter the market and compete.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>This describes another potential way that AI could help individuals.</li></ul></li></ul>","All humans are innately evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Humans are innately evil, and the often accompanying claim that this evil must then rectified in some way.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a philosophy thesis for many different philosophies across the world and through time, most prominently, Hobbes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This fact is often cited when justifying a powerful state or otherwise some kind of cultural force which would be able to rectify this issue inherent in humans. For instance, this is the fundamental axiom Hobbes employs in order to justify his Leviathan. Between the Confucianist schools and Legalist schools, there is a persistent tension between a sort of idealism regarding human nature and a stark realism. Confucianism argues for the moral uplifting of all, such that all would be able to harmoniously live among each other, while the Legalist school argues for the lowering of the moral standard and ruling people merely through totalitarian laws and regulations.</p><h5>Xunzi</h5><p>Argued that people are born evil, but that they are use their intellects in order to bring themselves out of their evil and into goodness. Specifically, through <em>li</em>, people are instructed to do the right things. Then, they are able to steer themselves towards what is right using their intellect. Eventually, through repeated practice, one becomes virtuous.</p><h5>Han Feizi</h5><p>Believed that while people are inherently evil, unlike Xunzi, that there is no mechanism for “saving” people. The only method, then, of running society, is to imbue a monarch with absolute authority and have them rule fairly and competently the rest of the population.</p><ul><li>The Taoists object to this, arguing that Legalism does not tell you how to become this ruler who is perfectly unselfish and impartial.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>This is the opposite thesis.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This is analogous to what Xunzi says.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>In both cases, it is existing morality, the formal guidelines (but especially in the case of the state) that does the teaching, analogous to the idea of <em>li</em>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>Referenced here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although humans have free will, our very fundamental “wants/wills” are something that transcends the individual, and is something that ultimately expresses a universal purpose that serves to drive history forward according to divine will.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Hegel’s view of the nature of history, and man’s place in it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although Caesar and Napoleon had the ability to do what they want, their actions remains, in its totality, in a divine system. Hegel terms this the <em>cunning of reason</em>.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that the justification for the existence of the cunning of reason is very weak. It is true that Caesar and Napoleon could not have known why they were doing what they were doing (i.e., they very underlying motivations) nor their impact on history. However, the evidence in support of the cunning of reason seems just as well to support fate or chance. In other words, the diagnosticity of the data in question is negligible.</p><h4>References</h4>","As history progresses, morality advances as well":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is moral progress in the world, and that we are fundamentally moving towards some ethical apex whereby humans will have uncovered the fundamental ethical “truths” extant in the universe.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is often assumed in contemporary discussions of the nature of morality, especially in the context of past moral wrongs.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that this view has heavy metaphysical implications, since it requires that there is in fact some indisputable moral truths and that we have in fact achieved moral progress towards it. It necessitates a response to several questions:</p><ul><li>How are we orienting our moral compasses correctly?</li><li>Why were humans originally developed in a state of moral ignorance?</li></ul><p>It seems that this idea is inextricable tried up in Christian theology, and the corresponding ideas of Original Sin and humanity’s progress towards God.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>This seems however to be a departure from Christian morality, since most moral progress as been associated with rebellion towards authority and status quo.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>This note is an example of this?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>Would capitalism be the force that holds back moral progress, or does capitalism stand as a testament against the very existence of some necessary moral truths?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism\">It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism</a><ul><li>It seems then that the idea of moral progress is incompatible with liberalism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ethics is grounded in religion\">Ethics is grounded in religion</a><ul><li>Related to the Christian morality thesis presented here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological\">All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological</a><ul><li>Maybe this is a hint of the ultimate morality.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>Related claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a></li></ul>","Berkson's paradox explains the existence of false negative correlations between two events":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Berkson’s paradox explains why there often seems to be a negative correlation between two independent variables due to selection bias.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may help us avoid falling into believing false dichotomies.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>There seems to be a negative correlation the attractiveness of a celebrity and their acting prowess. However, this is likely due to selection bias, as those who are neither attractive nor good at acting become actors. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Randomization allows for causation to be determined\">Randomization allows for causation to be determined</a><ul><li>This is a principle in statistics that is important to understand; since Berkson’s paradox is not random, causation cannot be determined.</li></ul></li><li>Designing a Good Experiment<ul><li>When designing a good experiment, make sure that the experience does not fall into the trap of Berkson’s paradox.</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Chinese society, due to their historically quick adaption of new technologies and the positive benefits of such adaptations, are generally more positive regarding new technologies.</p><ul><li>This explains their largely positive outlook towards AI and its potential. 83% of people are optimistic about AI in China, whereas only 39% of people are optimistic about it in the US.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the Chinese may receive the benefits of new technologies before other societies do.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some have argued that the cause of this positive attitude to technology is not necessarily a result of techno-optimism. Rather, it is because Chinese history has demonstrated that those who are not able to meet the pace of new technology and adopt it timely fall behind in society.</p><ul><li>This was what occurred in Northern China during the Reform and Opening Up, when millions of people working in government-run industries lost their jobs to the South and East of China, as higher tech industries sprang up.</li></ul><p>Either way, there is higher trust and willingness (whether it is out of genuine techno-optimism or simply FOMO) in China compared to the West. Another possible explanation for this phenomenon is the fact that in China, human lives are valued less, since there is an oversupply of human lives compared to the rest of the world (China has too many people).</p><ul><li>As a society that is not as rule-of-law-based as the US, protection for individual rights are correspondingly far weaker.</li><li>People care less when other people’s jobs are overtaken by AI, since human lives are valued less.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“Involution Without Export Is Wasted Effort”</p><p>When you live in a society so arbitrary, <strong>the only option is to make yourself adaptable.</strong> So when someone in Beijing tells me AI is inevitable and you might as well learn to live with it, I hear their parents’ lives in that sentence. Americans grow up assuming change is something you get a vote on. It doesn’t just “happen to you”, rather you get to decide if it even happens or not.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>Additionally, China’s strong state is able to ensure that new technologies are fostered by the central government.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that Christian morality is at some fundamental level, the closest to the a universal moral system, since it has so widely been adapted everywhere.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is used by some to argue for Christian morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In fact, many social movements that are avowedly Atheistic nonetheless adopt the morality associated with Christianity.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Couldn’t this just because of the large geopolitical influence that Europe has exerted across the world in the past, and not necessarily a result of the inherent appealingness of the moral system?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>It seems that capitalism has been able to coexist with Christian morality, but why?</li></ul></li></ul>","Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Concord is when people have the same opinion about what is to their interest, choose the same actions, and carry out what they decided in common.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for Aristotle’s discussions on politics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Concord specifically regards an alignment in belief of practical matters that shape life.<ul><li>It regards shared purpose, not just shared opinion.</li></ul></li><li>It is inherently political, concerning common advantage in the state.</li><li>It is only able to be reached by virtuous people, not vicious ones.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In order for a state to have concord, many people must be virtuous.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core of Confucian philosophy is centered around 义 and 仁, or righteousness (the “oughtness” in a situation) and human-heartedness (“loving others”).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the fundamental principles of Confucianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Righteousness describes categorical imperatives. Confucius theorizes that everyone in society has certain things that he ought to do, and these things ought to be done for their own sake, because it is the morally right thing to do. Even doing the right thing, if done with ulterior motives, makes an action no longer righteous.</p><ul><li>The concept of <em>yi</em> is opposed by the concept of <em>li</em> (利), or doing things for profit.</li></ul><p>Human-heartedness is the essential duty of “loving others,” which is the idea of treating others as one would want oneself to the treated. There are, more specifically, two aspects to this practice:</p><ul><li><em>Zhong</em> (忠), or the idea of being conscientious to others.</li><li><em>Shu</em> (恕)，or the idea of altruism.</li></ul><p>It is through practicing these two principles that one becomes “one with the universe,” and that “all things are complete within us (万物皆备于我).” <em>Ren</em> would be further applied to politics by Mencius, who argued that the government must act in accordance to this principle as well, not just individuals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a><ul><li>The principles of <em>zhong</em> and <em>shu</em> would be applied to the state by Mencius, and develop associated beliefs regarding the nature and legitimacy of the state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a charged item is brought next to a neutral conductor, the conductor is polarized (i.e., the side that is closer to the item accumulates opposite charges.) Such a polarized conductor is said to have an <em>induced charge</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Electrostatic induction will be on the AP test.</p><h4>Connections with Chemistry</h4><ul><li>The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Induction requires free electrons, which is why it needs a conductor.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule\">The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</a></li></ul>","Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Financial momentum is when due to a change in prices, further prices change occur. This is considered an anomaly in the efficient market hypothesis. This is also empirical evidence that financial momentum does have some relation with the winner of some bet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The fact that financial moment actually as has prediction power when it comes to prediction markets mean that prediction market are not perfect examples of the efficient-market hypothesis. And also, this has practical significance.</p><h4>Empirical Evidence</h4><p>From the bar graphs above, it is evident that financial moment does have a statistically significant impact on the outcome of a prediction market bet. What is interesting is that at some probabilities, a downward change in the daily move can actually lead to the a positive chance in the final result.</p><h4>References</h4>","Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Good work sets up relations between its elements, both within a level and across levels, and these recurrences hold the work together whether or not they carry any determinate meaning.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why symbolism can be effective without being interpretable, which is otherwise puzzling on any account that treats symbols as encoded messages.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The recurrence of the number three throughout Kafka’s <em>Metamorphosis</em>, and of ears throughout <em>Hamlet</em>, are examples where the pattern does structural work regardless of what it is taken to mean. The requirement pairs with surprise: echo without surprise is formula, and surprise without echo is noise.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art is surprising\">Great art is surprising</a><ul><li>The complementary criterion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content\">Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content</a><ul><li>A specific device by which echoes are produced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot\">A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot</a><ul><li>Layering across scales rather than repetition within one.</li></ul></li></ul>","Happiness is linearly related to log(income), not just income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Happiness is linearly related to log(income), meaning that the same absolute change in income derives a real but increasingly smaller amount of additional happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the correlation between income and happiness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This correlation seems to exist wherever you look, including within countries, between countries, and between historical time periods. There are some exceptions to the pattern, such as hunter gatherers, who had a surprisingly high level of happiness for the amount of real wealth that they owned.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>This seems to undermine Aristotle? Or maybe, since the results of the studies merely prove a correlation and not causation, what Aristotle says may be a potential confounder?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Instant-runoff voting (alternative voting) is a plurality voting system where the candidates are ranked. It solves the spoiler effect since people rank the party that they favor most as number one, and do not strategically vote by choosing the party that they dislike the least.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a significant advantage that instant-runoff voting has over standard SMP voting.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This being said, instant-runoff voting still shares many problems in common with SMP:</p><ul><li>Cannot prevent gerrymandering.</li><li>Is not proportional.</li><li>Does not elect the Condorcet Winner.</li><li>Both also trend towards two parties</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls' view of justice, he claims, would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons from his conception of the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds a contractarian element to Rawls' Justice as Fairness theory, which makes it vulnerable to critiques of those views.</p><h4>Explantion</h4><p>Rawls' view of the original position most prominently includes the veil of ignorance, which ensures fairness in the original position. The fact that this is fair results in the moral payoff of his system, which is that agents which are a part of the system mutually cooperate and engage with each other under the understanding that they were free and equal persons whose relations with respect to each other were fair.</p><ul><li>In this way, the society is almost voluntary, since it free and rational agents would indeed accept such a society based around justice as fairness.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>George F. Kennan’s “containment” policy which shaped American foreign policy during the Cold War was inspired by Spykman’s Rimland Thesis, in that Kennan wanted to prevent the Soviets from controlling as much of the Rimland as possible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This places the US policy of containment during the Cold War as it existed in the context of previous theories that would have justified a policy such as containment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world</a></li></ul>","Monopolies should make more money":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that is would both be more efficient (a given considering basic microeconomics) and more fair to have monopolies make more money, especially through price discrimination.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to address a common misconception today that price discrimination is always bad, since it reduces consumer surplus.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The author points to the fact that the use of things like coupons are technically price discrimination, and in that case, having a little bit of price discrimination is actually beneficial, since it also creates consumer surplus. This is better than the situation where the people who would otherwise not have purchased without the coupon does purchase, and thus both consumers have more surplus, and the monopolist has more surplus. Additionally, having more price discrimination means that the monopolist has more money that it is able to invest into R&amp;D, which may increase allowing for greater technological innovations. If it is the case that innovation is getting more and more difficult (which is backed up by empirical evidence), then firms will have to save up more and more of their surplus in order to actually make new innovations.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Brian Albrecht, <em>Monopolies don't make enough money</em></p><p>Without price discrimination, the profit from selling the good is not large enough to justify investing in the new product, so innovation does not occur. But price discrimination makes it profitable to innovate.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic\">An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic</a><ul><li>This describes the basic nature of the monopolist, and one of the reasons that monopolists are not efficient.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>A reason for the formation of a monopoly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>This is another potential barrier to innovation, that could potentially make innovation more difficult in the future.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>If this is the case, then maybe monopolists should remain more inefficient if they want to reduce inequality?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas are getting harder to find\">Ideas are getting harder to find</a><ul><li>The empirical evidence that ideas/innovation are getting more difficult to find.</li></ul></li></ul>","Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that objective truths in fact exist, and they can be arrived upon through rigorous applications of logic and the scientific method.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This forms the core of the pragmatist philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Peirce gives the following as proof that the objective (or as he terms it, “Reals”) exists*:</p><ol><li>If through scientific investigation, we cannot prove that there are Reals, it at least does not prove the opposite.</li><li>Nobody can doubt that when there are two claims which are contradictory to each other, that there must be one which is correct, or more correct.</li><li>The scientific method is already widely used to find Reals, except when people don’t know how to apply it.</li><li>Historically, the scientific method has been successful in finding Reals.</li></ol><h4>Note</h4><p>This is far more a social than logical argument, ironically. Most of these premises are, if considered strictly, fallacious.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>The comparative argument is stronger than the four premises above. Science is preferable to tenacity, authority and the a priori method not because it is certain but because it is the only one of the four that contains a mechanism for its own correction; the other three are stable precisely because nothing in them responds to being wrong.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science\">Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science</a><ul><li>The typology this conclusion is drawn from.</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One should choose to do the things that good for them (in their particular circumstances), not what is “good in themselves,” i.e., honor, wealth, power, pleasure, etc.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explicates Aristotle’s theory of virtue, as he says that the most important thing is that one cultivates their overall virtue, not the amount of something that one has.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The unjust person is one who pursues things that are “good in themselves,” and not necessarily what is “good for them” in the specific circumstances that they are bound to. In this way, since it is the pursuit of what is good for oneself at the specific moment and under specific circumstances that brings the individual virtue, by not engaging in such pursuits, but are no longer virtuous and thus become unjust.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>This means that one must pursue what is good for them, not what is good in itself.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>One should be seeking moral goodness, but technical goodness in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Only realized institutions, i.e., institutions which are effectively and impartially administered in reality, can be considered to be unjust or just. To judge an abstract institution means to judge the realized version of that institution.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that in order to consider some institution, we have to consider the way that it would function in reality (though still idealized), and not just consider it as an abstract concept.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>When judging this institution, we then must judge the realized role of this institution (perhaps though empirical evidence) and not the idealized, abstract version of the institution.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This may come from the fact that there is a discrepancy between the abstract institution and the realized institution.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America\">Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that when there is predicted to be a fiscal surplus, the risk of inflation is lowered.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explain a mechanism by which inflation is controlled.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This phenomenon is true for several reasons:</p><ol><li>There is overall less spending in the economy. When there is a surplus, it means that either government spending decreased, or tax rates increased. Either way, the total amount of money being spent in an economy is decreased, meaning that inflation is slowed.</li><li>When governments run fiscal surpluses, investors and the public view the government’s currency as backed by real future assets, i.e., that the government won’t have to print future money to pay off its debts. This makes the currency more valuable now, increasing its purchasing value, and driving down inflation. <ol><li>Note: this theory is predicated on the Fiscal Theory of the Price Level.</li></ol></li><li>When the previous theory is applied internationally, it means that the currency rises against other currencies (since it would be viewed as being more stable), meaning that the currency appreciates, and drives down inflation.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a><ul><li>Describes an impact of inflation, if it occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>When there is certainty (in this case, when the government is predicted to run a budget surplus), inflation is lowered, providing support to the claim above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Society corrupted innately good humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rousseau had the idea that humans are innately good, but society, specifically bad societal institutions, corrupted them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This connects with Rousseau’s other thoughts regarding moral and political philosophy, as well as his views on children and civilization.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rousseau’s views here play into his views regarding moral and political philosophy, as he believed that change needs to occur at the societal level, not the individual level, which connects to his idea of the social contract. He also viewed children in a much more benevolent light, arguing that education must be imparted on young people to prevent the corrupting influence of society.</p><h5><em>All The King’s Men</em></h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 217</p><p>“Man is conceived in sin and born in corruption and he passeth from the stink of the didie to the stench of the shroud. There is always something.”</p></blockquote><p>Although it might seem from the quote above that Stark views people as being innately evil, this is not the case, as can be seen from his own life. Stark began as something who was very innocent, but gradually developed into the ruthless dictator that he later became. This seems to have been a result of his exposure to the true nature of the world of politics, which required people to get their hands dirty in order to get what they want.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>This is the prerequisite for holding the believes outlined here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li>The opposite of this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Labor Theory of Value is false":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Labor Theory of Value is false.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a theory that is argued for my Marx, and forms the basis of Marxist economics.</p><h4>Counterarguments</h4><ol><li>“<strong>Intrinsic Value” is non-Popperian</strong>: The idea of Socially Necessary Labor Time as something that is only able to be considered through price is unfalsifiable. It is entirely constructed based on the prices observed in the market ex-post.<ul><li>A good that sells at a low price has a low SNLT, and a good that sells at a high price has a high SNLT. Both these are only determined after the fact, and thus can be defined as anything someone wants.</li></ul></li><li><strong>The Transformation Problem</strong>: <ul><li>Marx states the Law of Value: since profit only comes from “surplus value,” or laborers who are paid below their marginal product, a labor-intensive venture should have higher profit margins than a capital-intensive venture.</li><li>He also states the Law of Equal Profit: capital moves around the market until profit equalizes across all industries.</li><li>The problem is that how can every capitalist have the same amount of profit even if they have different amounts of labor use?</li><li>To solve this, Marx introduces the transformation, which says that firms that are more labor intensive are contributing to firms that are more capital intensive. <ul><li>He does this by defining the Price of Production: $Constant \\ Capital + Variable \\ Capital + Average \\ Profit$. And Value: $Constant \\ Capital + Variable \\ Capital + Surplus \\ Value$</li><li>When you subtract the two, labor heavy firms lose value, while capital heavy industries gain the value lost by the capital heavy industries (assuming that capital does not create value).</li></ul></li><li>The problem here is that the price of constant capital changes due to the that surplus value adds to the price of goods needed as raw materials for production. This means that any moment, the total price in the economy is greater than the total value, meaning that capital must have created value, disproving the labor theory of value.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Joint Production</strong>: When multiple goods are produced at the same time, any attempt to quantify the labor value between the goods produced at the same time is arbitrary.</li><li><strong>Homogenous Labor is false</strong>: Labor is not homogenous. The labor of a doctor and the labor of janitor cannot be quantified the same way.</li><li><strong>Natural Resources and Scarcity Rents:</strong> Factors such as the local supply of something also determines the price. A bottle of really rare wine, although it might have taken the same amount of labor as many other wines, is nonetheless valued more because it is rare.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a><ul><li>Typically, capital deepening is viewed as a way for economies to grow. Under the LVT, however, it seems actually constrict growth.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Upanishads teaches that all things are one in Brahman":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A collection of sacred texts composed by anonymous thinkers from around 800 to 400 B.C.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant texts for Hinduism.</p><h4>Contents</h4><p>Discusses the idea of the Brahman, the World Soul, and the final and ultimate reality, which is the primal unitary energy that infuses all things. Essentially, the idea that \"All Things Are One.\"</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman\">The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman</a></li></ul>","The standard CPI overestimates inflation, since it does not take into account the substitution effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The standard CPI measurement does not take into account the fact that consumers are able to substitute more expensive goods for cheaper ones (substitution effect), meaning that it overstates the effect of inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a downside to using the standard CPI as a measurement for the effects of inflation, a problem that the Chained CPI measure helps prevent.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When inflation occurs, prices become higher and consumers are able to purchase less of the same goods as before. However, after a certain point, they may switch to purchasing a different set of goods that result in a higher total utility given that they are a lower price. Where the consumption bundle of the individual was originally, and $q_{2}$ represents a composite good. Let us define this budget constraint as $X$:  $$q_{1}p_{1}+q_{2}\\leq m$$ Due to inflation, $p_{1}$ shifts to $p'<em>{1}$, such that $p'</em>{1} &gt; p_{1}$, meaning that either $q_{1}$ or $q_{2}$ or both have to shift down by some amount. For purposes of simplification, let us reduce $q_{2}$ to $q'<em>{2}$. Thus, the new budget constraint, $X</em>{2}$ is $$q_{1}p'<em>{1}+q'</em>{2}\\leq m$$ Say there is some third good $q_{3}$ that has the following budget constraint, $X_{3}$ if there consumer were to consume it: $$ q_{3}p_{3}+q_{2}\\leq m $$ If, say $X \\succ X_{3} \\succ X_{2}$, if inflation were to occur, the consumer would rationally decide to consume $X_{3}$ rather than $X_{2}$, thereby illustrating the effect of the substitution effect and the tendency for the CPI to overstate inflation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply\">A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","Trust measurement for the quality of institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The amount of trust in a society is a proxy measure which one can use to determine the effectiveness of the society’s institutions. The lower the trust, the lower the quality of institutions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the important of institutions in a society, since they are the foundations for the establishment of trust, and important factor in the way that societies function.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems to me a little of hard to have a causality measure in this case. Is it low trust that creates bad institutions, or is it bad institutions that lead to low trust? Or is it both at the same time?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a></li></ul>","Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that there are two different types of virtue: intellectual and moral. Intellectual virtues are ones which are taught, and can be forgotten if not used. Moral virtues come from habit (ethos), which means that you learn them through repeated practice, not studying theory. Thus, they become a permanent way that one engages with the world once established.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for Aristotle’s elucidation of, specifically moral virtue, in Book II.</p><h4>Moral Virtue</h4><p>Moral virtues are established through repeated practice, not just abstract study. The integration of physical practice into moral virtue is its big separator from intellectual virtue. We are constituted by nature to have a propensity for moral virtue, but their full development still requires repeated practice until it becomes a habit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Virtues are the key to happiness, which Aristotle says is the goal of life.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This explains what Aristotle means when he talks about being morally virtuous.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>An elaboration of the idea present in this note that virtue must be practiced repeatedly in order for it to manifest in one’s “disposition.”</li></ul></li></ul>","What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls deliberately give a thin and non-controversial definition of the good (since people in the original position do not know what they would consider good). Importantly, his definition of good incorporates rationality, and also are necessarily based on/need the primary goods, since the execution of a rational plan needs primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This theory of the good is what underlies all of Rawls’ other claims about what justice would be.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Aristotle could perhaps argue, based off of Rawls’ definition, that to be good means you will be happy? But this also makes sense, given that Rawls’ definition is an evolution of Aristotle’s.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>Rawls would argue that pleasure is in no way the good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a><ul><li>Rawls would agree.</li></ul></li></ul>","Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Wisdom (<em>sophia</em>) is the combination of the virtues of scientific knowledge and intellect. A person with wisdom both understands the first principles, and the necessary truths that follow from those first principles. It is directed at the most divine and superior objects in the universe (e.g., the gods, the cosmos, etc.), rather than human affairs. It allows the individual to search for truth, and it not aimed at increasing utility for the individual.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Wisdom is the highest of all of the intellectual virtues (even above prudence), and thus, it is the most desirable.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a><ul><li>One of the two components. Intellect supplies the first principles.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>The other component. Scientific knowledge reasons deductively from those first principles.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>While wisdom is not needed in order to achieve happiness, it <em>is</em> needed to achieve the <em>highest</em> happiness (a contemplative life).</li></ul></li></ul>"});
